# The Envelope in the Offering Plate

On a warm Sunday morning in the third week of July, in the fourth pew from the front on the pulpit side of the First Congregational Church of Chocorua, New Hampshire — the small white clapboard meetinghouse the town raised, one plank at a time, in the summer of 1792 — a seventy-two-year-old widow named Priscilla Whitcombe, Prissy to the three women she has known since Miss Coffin's second grade at Chocorua Village School in the fall of 1959, sits in the ladder-back pew Henry's grandmother refinished on the parsonage lawn in the summer of 1948.

The pew is the fourth from the front. Pulpit side. It has been the Whitcombe pew — in the plain unhurried arithmetic of a small New Hampshire church — since the January Sunday in 1897 when Henry's great-grandfather Elias L. Whitcombe, freshly widowed at forty-one, moved down from the third pew to sit with his two boys and never moved back.

Prissy sits at the aisle end.

The space to her left — the small warm space between her elbow and the frayed hymnal rack Henry's uncle Wendell varnished in the summer of 1962 — is empty.

It has been empty for eighty-eight Sundays.

## What she carries in the small handbag

In the small brown leather handbag on the pew beside her — the one Henry bought her at Filene's in Boston on their honeymoon in the June of 1972 — she carries the following.

A worn copy of the New Revised Standard Version, given to her by her mother in the June of 1968, with a small pressed daisy in the margin of the Song of Solomon.

A folded linen handkerchief, embroidered with the initials *H. L. W.,* which she has kept in the handbag since the Tuesday of Henry's memorial service in the second week of November 2019.

A pack of peppermint Life Savers, purchased on Friday at the small pharmacy on Main Street in Center Ossipee.

And, in the smaller inside pocket that closes with a small brass snap, a plain cream envelope, unsealed, addressed on the front — in her own round hand, in blue ink — to *The First Congregational Church of Chocorua.*

Inside the envelope, folded once, is a check for five hundred dollars.

It is dated the nineteenth of July.

On the memo line, in blue ink, in twenty-three careful characters — she counts them, on Saturday morning, at the small mahogany drop-leaf table by the window that overlooks Chocorua Lake:

*In loving memory of Henry.*

## What happens at ten forty-seven

At ten forty-five, the Reverend Amelia Ann Sweetser — fifty-one, ordained at Andover Newton in 2003, called to Chocorua from a small church in Waldoboro in the fall of 2017 — steps down from the pulpit, in the plain unhurried way of a Congregational minister who was raised in a small parsonage in the town of Union, Maine, and opens the small brass offering plates on the communion table.

At ten forty-six, the two ushers — Roger Prescott, sixty-eight, retired mail carrier, and his son Roger Jr., forty-two, currently the plumber for the town water department — step down the center aisle in the plain unhurried way of two men who have been walking down the center aisle of the First Congregational Church of Chocorua, on the third Sunday of the month, since Roger Sr. was a boy of nine in the summer of 1967.

The organist, Miss Peggy Whittaker — seventy-four, choir director since 1977, retired music teacher at Kennett High School in North Conway — plays the Doxology.

At three bars in, in the plain unrushed reading of a Vermont-born widow who has been sitting in the fourth pew on the pulpit side for fifty-four Julys, Prissy Whitcombe reaches into the small brown handbag, closes the small brass snap behind her, and slips the plain cream envelope, unsealed, into the small brass plate as Roger Jr. passes it, at ten forty-seven, along the ladder-back rail.

Roger Jr. does not look down.

He has been taught, since he was twelve, not to.

## What Thurston writes in the small hardbound ledger

Deacon Emeritus Thurston Peabody — eighty-one, retired postmaster of the Chocorua post office from 1971 to 2010, deacon since 1971, deacon emeritus since the June of 2016 — sits at the small oak desk in the sacristy behind the pulpit on Sunday afternoon at one-fifteen, with a cup of the black coffee his wife Vinnie has kept warm for him in the small thermos she bought at Ames in the fall of 1994, and — in the plain unhurried way of a New Hampshire postmaster who has been keeping ledgers since he was nineteen — counts the plate.

The ledger is a small hardbound cloth-covered notebook, the color of oxblood, the size of a hymnal. He has been keeping it since the summer of 1971.

The left column is the date.

The middle column is the plain undesignated giving. The offertory. What most people put in the plate: a five, a ten, a folded twenty, a small envelope from the young family in the front pew with a check for a hundred and twenty-five.

The right column, in Thurston's small careful hand, is what he calls *the memorial line.*

For fifty-four years, on the third Sunday of every month, Thurston has entered, in the right column, in the plain unhurried arithmetic of a Chocorua postmaster, the sums the town's widows and widowers have set into the plate in memory of the people they married in this building.

For eighty-eight Sundays — every Sunday since the third Sunday of the second week of November 2019 — Thurston has entered, in the right column, on the line for the Whitcombe pew, the same figure in the same round hand:

*$500. Whitcombe, P. — in memory of H. L. W.*

By the third week of July 2026, in the plain unhurried arithmetic of Thurston Peabody's small oxblood ledger, Prissy Whitcombe has, since the November of 2019, put forty-four thousand dollars into the offering plate at the First Congregational Church of Chocorua, New Hampshire, in her late husband's name.

She has done it in a plain cream envelope. Every Sunday. At ten forty-seven. Three bars into the Doxology.

## What the alumni file says on Monday

Somewhere on the third floor of a converted brick stable on the campus of a small women's college in Poughkeepsie, New York — a college Prissy attended from the fall of 1968 to the June of 1972, on the strength of a small scholarship the college awarded her because her father was a career forest ranger in the White Mountain National Forest and could not, in the honest reading of the family finances in 1968, have paid the tuition — on Monday morning at nine-oh-two, the record for Priscilla H. Whitcombe, class of 1972, reads as follows.

*Address:* Route 16, Chocorua, New Hampshire.
*Class:* 1972.
*Degree:* B.A., art history, 1972.
*Spouse:* Henry L. Whitcombe (deceased 2019).
*Giving since:* 1974.
*Last gift:* October 12, 2019. $50.
*Status:* Lapsed.
*Interests:* Class notes, Chapel Choir, the small library annex.

There is no field on the record for *the fourth pew on the pulpit side of the First Congregational Church of Chocorua, New Hampshire.*

There is no field on the record for *the plain cream envelope, unsealed, in the small brass snap of the handbag Henry bought her at Filene's in June of 1972.*

There is no field on the record for *the twenty-three characters, in blue ink, on the memo line of Saturday morning's check.*

There is no field on the record for *the small oxblood ledger, in the plain careful hand of Thurston Peabody, in the sacristy at one-fifteen on Sunday afternoon, since 1971.*

The fall appeal will land in Prissy's mailbox at the small post office in Chocorua Village on the second Tuesday of October. It will be addressed *Dear Alumna.* It will not mention Henry. It will not mention the fourth pew. It will not mention the eighty-eight Sundays she has kept the memorial line in the small oxblood ledger, in the plain careful hand of Thurston Peabody, in her husband's name.

Prissy will read the first paragraph.

She will put it, still open, on the mahogany drop-leaf table by the window.

She will not write the check.

## The ledger is the annual report

Every advancement office in America publishes a lapsed-donor report. It is generated on Monday morning by a wealth-screening subscription that costs eight thousand dollars a year. It is filtered by *last gift date* and *estimated capacity.* It marks Priscilla H. Whitcombe, class of 1972, with a small yellow flag that reads *low priority — dormant, low four-figure ceiling.*

Down the street from that widow, in the small white clapboard meetinghouse the town raised in the summer of 1792, a plain cream envelope has been landing in the small brass offering plate at ten forty-seven, three bars into the Doxology, on every Sunday since November of 2019. It is unsealed. It carries a check for five hundred dollars. It bears, on the memo line, in twenty-three careful characters, the plain unhurried reading of a widow who has not, for one Sunday in eighty-eight, forgotten to give.

The ledger is the annual report.

The annual report is not.

We have been reading the wrong document about the wrong donor for eighty-eight Sundays.

## What Rōmy is doing about it

Rōmy does not sit in the fourth pew of the First Congregational Church of Chocorua at ten forty-seven on the third Sunday of July.

It does not fold the plain cream envelope, unsealed, at the small mahogany drop-leaf table on Saturday morning at eight-fifteen, and it is not going to count the plate, at one-fifteen on Sunday afternoon, with Thurston Peabody in the sacristy.

What Rōmy does is smaller than that. It is a *reading.*

Rōmy reads the small papers. All of them. The *Carroll County Independent,* the *Conway Daily Sun,* the *Mountain Ear,* the *Ossipee Valley Advertiser,* the *Kearsarge Shopper,* the *Bethlehem Chronicle,* and the small quarterly newsletter of the First Congregational Church of Chocorua — the one Miss Peggy Whittaker types on the Sunday afternoon after the third Sunday of every month, on the old Selectric IBM in the church office, and mails to two hundred and forty households, from the little post office annex under the maple in Chocorua Village.

It reads, in the *Church News* section on page two of the church's quarterly newsletter, the small paragraph in nine-point Times that begins:

*Memorial gifts received this quarter, with grateful thanks to the families of our beloved…*

It cross-references — quietly, in the corner of the associate director of advancement's screen at eight-forty on a Monday morning in Poughkeepsie — every name against the CRM.

On the Monday morning after Miss Peggy's Sunday-afternoon newsletter, Rōmy puts, at the top of the associate director's list, a small quiet note:

*Priscilla H. Whitcombe, class of 1972 (B.A., art history, June 1972; Chapel Choir alto, 1969–72), longtime donor from 1974 to 2019 (last gift, Homecoming appeal, October 12, 2019, $50 — three weeks before her husband's passing on November 4, 2019), widow of Henry L. Whitcombe (1946–2019), has been placing, on every Sunday since the third Sunday of November 2019, a plain cream envelope in the offering plate at the First Congregational Church of Chocorua, New Hampshire — a check for $500, memo line reading "In loving memory of Henry." Deacon Emeritus Thurston Peabody (postmaster, Chocorua, 1971–2010; deacon since 1971) has been recording the memorial line, in his own hand, in a small oxblood ledger in the sacristy since 1971. The church's quarterly newsletter, typed by Peggy Whittaker (organist since 1977), thanks Prissy by name in the Memorials section of page two, this quarter and every quarter since Winter 2019. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand, to Route 16, Chocorua — addressing Priscilla as Prissy (per Chapel Choir yearbook, 1971), mentioning Henry by first name, thanking her — quietly, in a sentence she will remember — for the eighty-eight Sundays she has kept the memorial line in Chocorua in his name. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

The card goes out on Tuesday.

Prissy reads it on Thursday, at the drop-leaf table by the window, with the second cup of the Green Mountain roast she has been buying by the pound at the small grocery on Chocorua Lake Road since 2004.

By November her fall appeal — addressed to *Dear Prissy,* with a handwritten postscript in the director's own blue-ink hand that reads *and thank you, for Henry, for the eighty-eighth Sunday* — comes back in a #10 envelope with a hand-cancelled stamp from the little post office annex under the maple in Chocorua Village. Inside is a check, dated the second week of November, made out to the college. The memo line, in blue ink, in seventeen careful characters:

*For Henry's pew.*

The plate was never the point. The ledger was never the point. The Doxology was never the point.

The *reading* was the point.

## A small assignment, with love ♡

This week, before anything else, do one small unfashionable thing.

Pick, from your lapsed-donor list, the three whose mailing address is a small New England town — a town with a white clapboard church, a village post office annex, and a quarterly newsletter you have never opened. If you cannot remember which three, pick the three whose last gift arrived in the ninety days before a spouse's obituary ran in the alumnae magazine.

Go to the website of the small Congregational, Episcopal, or Methodist church whose steeple you can see in the Google Street View of the donor's mailing address. If the church does not have a website — and about a third of the small New England churches, in the Whites and the Berkshires and the Green Mountains, still do not — call the church office and ask, in your kindest voice, whether the church secretary would mail you the last four quarterly newsletters. She will. She has been waiting for a reason to.

Turn to page two. Read the *Memorial Gifts* paragraph. Read it slowly, in the plain unhurried way Miss Peggy Whittaker types it on the old Selectric IBM in the small church office in Chocorua on Sunday afternoons in the third week of every quarter.

If a name you know is set in nine-point Times — a donor, a donor's late husband, a donor's family — take a plain cream Crane's card from the top drawer of the credenza.

Do not put a case for support in it.

Do not put a QR code in it.

Do not sign it with a title.

Write three sentences, in blue ink, in your own hand. Say her name once. Say his name once. Say the small specific thing the church printed on page two — the eighty-eighth Sunday, the fourth pew, the plain cream envelope — and say, in the plain language of a person who was paying attention, that you read it in the memorial paragraph.

Walk it, on your lunch break, to the post office on your corner.

The plate came back to the communion table on Sunday morning at ten fifty-two.

The plain cream envelope is still in the small oxblood ledger, in the sacristy, in the plain careful hand of Thurston Peabody, since 1971.

Miss Peggy has been holding the memorial paragraph, in the small church office at the Selectric IBM, since 1977.

Turn to it. ♡
